\section{Introduction}

The main purpose of the article is to find empirical based answers to the question “Under what conditions is deforestation associated with improved local living conditions?”

Deforestation has historically expanded humanity’s capacity to produce food, timber, and other goods. Converting forests to cropland, pasture, plantations, etc can create economic opportunities and contribute to improved material living conditions. At the same time, forest loss entails substantial environmental costs, including reductions in biodiversity, the release of greenhouse gases, and the loss of ecosystem services provided by standing forests.

Some of the benefits generated by forest conversion may accrue directly to local populations through increased access to farmland. Other benefits, particularly from agricultural and forestry production integrated into national or international markets, may accrue primarily to actors outside the local area. Conversely, many of the ecosystem services provided by intact forests may generate benefits at regional or global scales rather than being directly captured by local populations. On the one hand, there is potential tension between local economic incentives to convert forests and broader incentives to conserve them and on the other hand there is a potential tension between the local use of the ecosystem services provided by forest and non-local economic interest that can extract value from deforestation.

Understanding these tensions are particularly important in regions where local populations face substantial material deprivation and where forest conversion may represent one of the available pathways to economic development. If deforestation is associated with improved local living conditions in some contexts but not in others, identifying conditions that may predict when forest conversion is associated with local economic gains and when those gains appear limited or non-existant is important.

This article addresses the question: Under what conditions is deforestation associated with improved local living conditions? We focus in particular on one characteristic of the post-deforestation economic landscape: the type of land use that replaces forest. We first examine where deforestation is associated with improvements, stability, or deterioration in local living conditions. We then assess whether post-deforestation land use helps explain this variation.
By shifting attention from the average relationship between deforestation and well-being to the conditions under which this relationship varies, the article seeks to provide a more differentiated empirical understanding of the socioeconomic consequences of forest loss.

\subsection{Research questions}
\label{sec:research-questions}

\begin{enumerate}
\item Where is deforestation associated with improved living conditions for local population and where is deforestation associated with stable or worsened living conditions for the local population?
\item Does data about what replaced the forest have a predictive power of whether or not deforestation will improve local living conditions?
\end{enumerate}

\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:setting-context}

The countries included in this study are those low- and middle-income countries that have been covered with geocoded household surveys, and the selection is driven by the availability of data on living conditions, not by the state of their forests. Many of today's high-income countries cleared much of their forest at earlier stages of their development \citep{owid-global-forest-transition}, and we have not studied deforestation in high-income countries in the study period. The combination of high quality time series data on forest coverage and geocoded household surveys is available from the year 2000 and up to 2020 there is good coverage.

Among the countries covered by the DHS, forest loss 2000--2020 was larger where GNI per capita was lower (Figure \ref{fig:context.countries}).

<<context.countries, fig.cap = "Forest loss 2000--2020 by GNI per capita in 2000, for the countries in the study with at least 1 per cent forest cover in 2000. Forest loss is the share of the forest area of 2000 that was not forest in 2020 \\citep[GLCLU,][]{Potapov2022}, summed over the administrative areas with geocoded DHS surveys. Point size is proportional to the forest area in 2000. The line is a quadratic fit in log GNI.", fig.width = 5, fig.height = 3.4, fig.align = "center", cache.extra = tools::md5sum(c("forest_transitions_2000_2020.csv", "GNI.RDS"))>>=
library(data.table); library(ggplot2); library(globallivingconditions)

## Forest loss per country, from the area-level transitions (transitions-per-area.R).
## The first field of the area name is the ISO 3166 numeric country code.
ctx.tr <- fread("forest_transitions_2000_2020.csv", encoding = "UTF-8")[valid_2000 > 0]
ctx.tr[, country := iso.3166$String[match(sub("\\..*$", "", region), as.character(iso.3166$numeric))]]
ctx.dt <- ctx.tr[!is.na(country), list(land_2000 = sum(valid_2000), forest_2000 = sum(forest_2000),
                                       loss = sum(forest_2000 - forest__forest)), by = country]
ctx.dt[, `:=`(forest_share_2000 = 100 * forest_2000 / land_2000, loss_pct = 100 * loss / forest_2000)]
ctx.dt <- ctx.dt[forest_share_2000 >= 1]

## GNI per capita in 2000 (nearest available year). India is by state in GNI.RDS;
## unless a national value is present, use the mean of the log GNI of the states.
ctx.gni <- as.data.table(readRDS("GNI.RDS"))[, list(country, year = year.of.interview, gdp.log)]
if (!"India" %in% ctx.gni$country)
    ctx.gni <- rbind(ctx.gni, ctx.gni[grepl("^India\\.", country),
                                      list(country = "India", gdp.log = mean(gdp.log, na.rm = TRUE)), by = year])
ctx.gni <- ctx.gni[!is.na(gdp.log) & !grepl("^India\\.", country)]
ctx.gni <- ctx.gni[order(abs(year - 2000))][, .SD[1], by = country]
ctx.dt <- merge(ctx.dt, ctx.gni[, list(country, gni_year = year, gni = exp(gdp.log))], by = "country")
ctx.dt[, label := iso.3166$String.code[match(country, iso.3166$String)]]
ctx.dt[is.na(label), label := country]
ctx.rho <- cor(ctx.dt$gni, ctx.dt$loss_pct, method = "spearman")

ggplot(ctx.dt, aes(gni, loss_pct)) +
    geom_hline(yintercept = 0, colour = "#c3c2bd", linewidth = 0.3) +
    geom_smooth(method = "lm", formula = y ~ poly(x, 2), se = TRUE, colour = "#2a78d6",
                fill = "#b7d3f6", linewidth = 0.6) +
    geom_point(aes(size = forest_2000), shape = 21, fill = "#2a78d6", colour = "#ffffff",
               stroke = 0.3, alpha = 0.85) +
    ggrepel::geom_text_repel(aes(label = label), size = 1.9, colour = "#52514e",
                             segment.size = 0.2, segment.colour = "#c3c2bd", max.overlaps = 30, seed = 1) +
    scale_x_log10(breaks = c(250, 500, 1000, 2000, 4000, 8000, 16000), labels = scales::comma) +
    scale_size_area(max_size = 6, guide = "none") +
    labs(x = "GNI per capita in 2000 (log scale)", y = "Forest loss 2000-2020 (% of forest in 2000)") +
    theme_minimal(base_size = 8) +
    theme(panel.grid.minor = element_blank(), panel.grid.major = element_line(colour = "#eeede9"),
          axis.title = element_text(colour = "#0b0b0b"), axis.text = element_text(colour = "#52514e"))
@

The rank correlation between GNI per capita and forest loss across the \Sexpr{nrow(ctx.dt)} countries is \Sexpr{sprintf("%.2f", ctx.rho)} (Spearman). The association also holds between administrative areas within countries and top level regions, when the IWI and the forest cover of the area are controlled for (figure \ref{fig:kuznet-7-effects}, Appendix \ref{app:gni-area}). It is an association among the low- and middle-income countries covered by the DHS, and nothing more: it does not show that a certain GNI per capita \emph{causes} deforestation, nor how deforestation varies over the full range of national incomes. Why the association exists is beyond the scope of this study. Our focus is the consequences of deforestation for those who live in areas where forest is replaced by something else, or more generally, how changes in forest cover relate to changes in local living conditions.


