\appendix

\section{GNI and deforestation at the area level}
\label{app:gni-area}

Figure \ref{fig:context.countries} compares countries. To check that the association between GNI and deforestation is not driven by a few countries, we also model it at the level of administrative areas. The data set has one row per area and year 2000, 2005, 2010 and 2015. The outcome is the change in forest cover over the following five years, in percentage points, from the forest cover per area every fifth year \citep{Ekbrand2026forest}. The predictors are log GNI per capita and its square; the average IWI of the area in year $t$, interpolated with a linear mixed model of IWI by area and interview year (random intercepts and slopes for area, top level region and country, controlling for the education of a fixed birth cohort); the forest cover of the area at time $t$; and year. The model has random intercepts for country and top level region. GNI per capita is imputed GNI from a previous study, with Indian states as separate units. % TODO: cite the GNI source

<<kuznet-7-effects, fig.cap = "Predicted forest cover change the next five years ($t+5$, percentage points) as a function of GNI per capita, from a linear mixed model with a linear and a quadratic term for log GNI, the average IWI of the area, the forest cover of the area at time $t$, year, and random intercepts for country and top level region. Shaded: 95\\% confidence interval.", fig.width = 5, fig.height = 3, fig.align = "center">>=
library(lme4); library(effects); library(lattice); library(latticeExtra)
my.fit.deforestation.7 <- lmer(forestation_change_5yr_forward ~ iwi + forest_coverage + gdp.log + I(gdp.log^2) + year + (1 | country), data = my.dt.clean)
my.fit.deforestation.7b <- lmer(forestation_change_5yr_forward ~ iwi + forest_coverage + gdp.log + I(gdp.log^2) + year + (1 | country) + (1 | top.level.region), data = my.dt.clean)

gdp_range <- range(my.dt.clean$gdp.log, na.rm = TRUE)
my.eff <- Effect("gdp.log", my.fit.deforestation.7b,
                 xlevels = list(gdp.log = seq(gdp_range[1], gdp_range[2], length.out = 100)))
## Hint to the exponentiation, but include 12000 as a upper point with reasonable coverage.
x_ticks <- c(1000, 2000, 4000, 8000, 12000)
p <- plot(my.eff,
          axes = list(x = list(gdp.log = list(lab = "GNI"), cex = 0.5),
                      y = list(cex = 0.5)),
          main = "", ylim = c(-1.3, 0.6))
p <- update(p,
            xlab = list(label="GNI", cex = 0.8),
            ylab = list(label="Predicted forestation change", cex = 0.8),
            scales = list(x = list(at = log(x_ticks), labels = x_ticks )))
p + 
    layer(panel.grid(h = -1, v = 0, lty = 2, lwd = 1, col = rgb(0.7, 0.7, 0.7, alpha = 1)), under = FALSE) + 
    layer(panel.abline(v = log(x_ticks), lty = 2, lwd = 1, col = rgb(0.7, 0.7, 0.7, alpha = 1)), under = FALSE) + 
    layer(panel.abline(h = 0, lty = 2, lwd = 1.2), under = FALSE)

@

The model is based on \Sexpr{format(nobs(my.fit.deforestation.7b), big.mark = ",")} area-years. Roughly speaking, in these low- and middle-income countries a GNI per capita above \$8000 per year is associated with no deforestation, while lower GNI is associated with deforestation (Figure \ref{fig:kuznet-7-effects}).
% The file final.RDS it is created automatically by the function \textrm{download.and.harmonise()} in the package \textrm{globallivingconditions}. The invokation of \textrm{download.and.harmonise()} was done by the template \textrm{dhs-debug-child.Rnw} which dfines the parameters used. As for parameters, the only non-default parameters used are

% \begin{verbatim}
%     file.types.to.download = c("PR", "GE", "IR", "MR", "KR"),
%     variable.packages = c("wealth", "india"),
% \end{verbatim}

% which means: ``get all file types, including the GIS ones'' and ``calculate IWI, include some special stuff for India''. The India stuff is to (a) create an alterantive country variable based on the indian state name and (b) use historical state division so that the set of states is constant, ie. undo state splits that occured after 1990 and (c) include a variable for the household weight within the indian state.

\section{Model specifications and variables}
\label{sec:model-specs}
\label{app:model-specs}

This section gives the equations of the model for research question 1 (Section \ref{sec:res-rq1}) and of the two-step models for research question 2 (Section \ref{sec:res-rq2}), and describes the variables, their sources and how they were scaled or transformed. Indices: $a$ is an administrative area, $r(a)$ its top level region, $c(a)$ its country, and $t$ an interview year with DHS data in the area.

\subsection{Model for research question 1}
\label{app:rq1-model}

The model in Section \ref{sec:res-rq1} is a linear mixed model of the area-year averages $\bar Y_{at}$ with interview years 2000--2020, estimated by restricted maximum likelihood with \texttt{lme4::lmer} \citep{Bates2015lme4}. The random effects and the residual are
\begin{gather*}
u_a \sim N(0, \sigma^2_u), \quad
\begin{pmatrix} v_r \\ b_r \end{pmatrix} \sim N(\mathbf{0}, \Sigma_r), \quad
d_r \sim N(0, \sigma^2_d), \quad
\begin{pmatrix} w_c \\ g_c \end{pmatrix} \sim N(\mathbf{0}, \Sigma_c), \quad
h_c \sim N(0, \sigma^2_h), \\
\varepsilon_{at} \sim N\!\left(0, \frac{\sigma^2}{\omega_{at}}\right),
\end{gather*}
with $\omega_{at} = n_{at}/\bar n$, where $n_{at}$ is the number of respondents behind the area-year average and $\bar n$ its mean over all area-years. The effect of forest loss in region $r$ of country $c$ is estimated as $\hat\beta_2 + \hat h_c + \hat d_r$, where $\hat h_c$ and $\hat d_r$ are the conditional modes, and its variance is approximated by $\mathrm{Var}(\hat\beta_2)$ plus the conditional variances of $h_c$ and $d_r$ (covariances are ignored). Whether the effect of forest loss varies is tested by comparing the model with one where $\sigma^2_d = \sigma^2_h = 0$, with a likelihood ratio test on the REML criteria (both models have the same fixed effects).

\subsection{Two-step models for research question 2}

\paragraph{Step 1: observed IWI trend per area (Section \ref{sec:obs-trend}).} Again with area-years as units, but on the original scales and with fixed effects (\texttt{fixest::feols}, \citealt{Berge2018fixest}):
\begin{equation}
\bar Y_{at} = \alpha_a + s_a (t - 2010) + \lambda\, e_{at} + \gamma_{c(a),t} + \varepsilon_{at}, \qquad
\mathrm{Var}(\varepsilon_{at}) = \tau^2_v + \frac{\sigma^2_w}{n_{at}}, \label{eq:step1}
\end{equation}
where $\alpha_a$ and $s_a$ are an intercept and a slope for each area, $\lambda$ is common to all areas, and $\gamma_{c,t}$ are fixed effects for country $\times$ interview year (DHS rounds). The variance of an area-year average has a between-village component $\tau^2_v$ and a between-household component $\sigma^2_w / n_{at}$; both are estimated from the leverage-corrected squared residuals of an unweighted first fit, and \eqref{eq:step1} is then estimated with weights $1/(\hat\tau^2_v + \hat\sigma^2_w / n_{at})$. With the country $\times$ year effects, $s_a$ is identified only relative to the other areas in the country, so the outcome of step 2 is the deviation from the inverse-variance weighted country mean,
\[
\tilde s_a = \hat s_a - \frac{\sum_{a' \in c(a)} \hat s_{a'} / \mathrm{se}_{a'}^2}{\sum_{a' \in c(a)} 1 / \mathrm{se}_{a'}^2},
\qquad
\mathrm{se}_a = \Big( \sum_t \hat\omega_{at} (t - \bar t_a)^2 \Big)^{-1/2},
\]
with $\hat\omega_{at} = 1/(\hat\tau^2_v + \hat\sigma^2_w / n_{at})$ and $\bar t_a$ the $\hat\omega$-weighted mean interview year of the area. Only areas with at least two interview years (after dropping country-years with a single area) have a trend.

\paragraph{Step 2: meta-regression.} One observation per area:
\begin{equation}
\tilde s_a = \sum_j \beta_j L_{aj} + \sum_m \gamma_m Z_{am} + \alpha_{g(a)} + \eta_a + e_a, \qquad
\eta_a \sim (0, \tau^2_{\mathrm{res}}), \quad e_a \sim (0, \mathrm{se}_a^2), \label{eq:step2}
\end{equation}
where $L_{aj}$ are the seven categories of forest loss $\{P, U, K, G, S, L, R\}$, $Z_{am}$ the controls $B$, $D$, $N^{\mathrm{bu}}$, $N^{\mathrm{cr}}$, $N^{\mathrm{op}}$, $U^{\mathrm{rb}}$, $F$ and $\hat\alpha^{2010}_a$, $\alpha_{g(a)}$ fixed effects for a group $g$, $\eta_a$ the variation in true trends between areas that the model does not explain, and $e_a$ the sampling error of the area's trend. The model is estimated by weighted least squares with weights $1/(\mathrm{se}_a^2 + \hat\tau^2_{\mathrm{res}})$, where $\hat\tau^2_{\mathrm{res}}$ is the \citet{PauleMandel1982} estimate (the value for which the weighted sum of squared residuals equals its degrees of freedom). Standard errors are clustered by country.

\begin{description}
\item[Model (1):] $g = $ top level region, weights $1/(\mathrm{se}_a^2 + \hat\tau^2_{\mathrm{res}})$ with $\hat\tau^2_{\mathrm{res}}$ estimated for this model.
\item[Model (2):] $g = $ country, with the weights of model (1). It differs from model (4) only in $\hat\tau^2_{\mathrm{res}}$ and is not shown.
\item[Model (3):] $g = $ top level region, unweighted (ordinary least squares).
\item[Model (4):] $g = $ country, weights $1/(\mathrm{se}_a^2 + \hat\tau^2_{\mathrm{res}})$ with $\hat\tau^2_{\mathrm{res}}$ estimated for this model. This is the main model.
\item[Model (5):] as model (4), unweighted.
\item[Model (6):] as model (4), restricted to areas whose interviews span at least ten years.
\item[Models (7) and (8):] as model (4), with $P$ split by plantation type (industrial, smallholder) or by planting period (2001--2010, 2011--2020), and oil palm planted before 2001 (on any land) as an additional control.
\end{description}

\paragraph{Reported quantities.} Tables \ref{tab:two-step-country} and \ref{tab:two-step} report $\beta_j \cdot s_{L_j}$, the difference in the observed IWI trend in IWI units per year associated with one standard deviation more of category $L_j$ (standard deviation across the areas in the model). Figure \ref{fig:rq2.coef} and Tables \ref{tab:palm-split} and \ref{tab:palm-split-contrast} report estimates and differences $\beta_j - \beta_k$ per percentage point of the land area.

\subsection{Variables}

All land cover variables are computed per area with coverage-fraction weighting, using the R package \texttt{exactextractr} \citep{exactextractr}: each 30 m cell counts with the fraction of the cell that lies inside the area. ``Land area'' excludes open water, ocean and cells with no data in 2000 (GLCLU codes 200--207, 254, 255). Unless stated otherwise, land cover variables are per cent of the land area in 2000 and are not otherwise transformed.

