\paragraph{Sources.} DHS: Demographic and Health Surveys \citep{Wang2021}, household and individual recode files with GPS coordinates, harmonised and linked to the administrative areas with the R package \texttt{globallivingconditions} \citep{Ekbrand_dhsharmonisation}; IWI as defined by \citet{Smits2014}. GLCLU: Global Land Cover and Land Use Change 2000--2020, v2 \citep{Potapov2022}, 30 m, years 2000 and 2020. Drivers: Global drivers of forest loss at 1 km resolution, v1.3, 2001--2025 \citep{Sims2025drivers}. Oil palm: global oil palm extent 2021 and planting year 1990--2021 \citep{Descals2024}. Administrative areas: the partition of the forest change data set \citep{Ekbrand2026forest}, based on GADM \citep{Hijmans_GADM_v41} with areas merged to ensure DHS coverage.

\clearpage

\section{Observed IWI trend per area}
\label{sec:obs-trend}

The education coefficient is \Sexpr{sprintf("%.2f", coef(fit.obs)[["average.education"]])} IWI units per year of education (s.e.\ \Sexpr{sprintf("%.2f", fixest::se(fit.obs)[["average.education"]])}). The estimated variance components are $\hat\tau = \Sexpr{sprintf("%.2f", sqrt(tau2.obs))}$ (between villages) and $\hat\sigma_w = \Sexpr{sprintf("%.1f", sqrt(sigma2w.obs))}$ (between households), in IWI units. Table \ref{tab:obs-trend-span} shows how the precision of the trends depends on the time between the first and the last interview year.

All areas in a country are interviewed in the same DHS rounds, so a round that deviates

The between-area standard deviation of the true IWI slopes within countries is \Sexpr{sprintf("%.3f", tau.within.country)} IWI units per year (95\% CI \Sexpr{sprintf("%.3f", tau.within.country.ci[["lower"]])}--\Sexpr{sprintf("%.3f", tau.within.country.ci[["upper"]])}); after the explanatory variables it is \Sexpr{sprintf("%.3f", sqrt(tau2.res.country))} (95\% CI \Sexpr{sprintf("%.3f", tau.res.country.ci[["lower"]])}--\Sexpr{sprintf("%.3f", tau.res.country.ci[["upper"]])}). Table \ref{tab:two-step-country} shows the estimates per standard deviation of each category, and Figure \ref{fig:rq2.coef} the differences from shifting cultivation per percentage point of the land area.

\section{Two-step models with fixed effects for top level regions}
\label{sec:two-step}

The between-area standard deviation of the true IWI slopes within top level regions is \Sexpr{sprintf("%.3f", tau.within.region)} IWI units per year, compared with \Sexpr{sprintf("%.3f", sqrt(tau2.slope))} across all areas (including differences between countries); after the explanatory variables it is \Sexpr{sprintf("%.3f", sqrt(tau2.res.region))}. Table \ref{tab:two-step} shows the estimates per standard deviation of each category.

<<app.two.step.table, results = "asis", cache = FALSE>>=
## as chunk two.step.table, without model (2)
app.two.step.tab <- data.table(variable = q2.short[q2.vars],
                               sapply(q2.fits[c(1, 3)], function(f) sapply(q2.vars, function(v) est.ci.q2(f, v, cat.sd[[v]]))))
setnames(app.two.step.tab, c("", "(1)", "(3)"))
xtable::print.xtable(xtable::xtable(app.two.step.tab,
             caption = paste0("Difference in the observed IWI trend (IWI units per year) associated with one standard deviation more of each category of forest loss 2000--2020, comparing areas within the same top level region, with 95\\% confidence intervals clustered by country. (1) weighted, (3) unweighted. ",
                              format(nrow(q2.dt), big.mark = ","), " areas. Weights $1/(\\mathrm{se}^2 + \\tau^2_{\\mathrm{res}})$ with $\\hat\\tau_{\\mathrm{res}} = ",
                              sprintf("%.3f", sqrt(tau2.res.region)),
                              "$. Controls as in Table \\ref{tab:two-step-country}. Model (2) is not shown, see Appendix \\ref{app:model-specs}."),
             label = "tab:two-step", align = c("l", "l", "r", "r")),
      include.rownames = FALSE, booktabs = TRUE, size = "footnotesize",
      sanitize.text.function = identity, sanitize.colnames.function = identity)
@

\section{Sensitivity of the within-country results}
\label{app:sensitivity}

Oil palm is concentrated in few areas and countries, so a single country could drive the results for P. We therefore re-estimate model (4) of Table \ref{tab:two-step-country} leaving out one country at a time. We also split category R, which mixes very different processes, into its components.

The five countries with most of the oil palm planted on former forest land account for \Sexpr{sprintf("%.0f", 100 * sum(head(palm.by.country$share, 5)))}\% of it (\Sexpr{paste(sprintf("%s %.0f\\%%", latex.escape(head(palm.by.country$country, 5)), 100 * head(palm.by.country$share, 5)), collapse = ", ")}). Leaving out one country at a time, the contrast P $-$ S ranges from \Sexpr{sprintf("%.4f", min(loo.dt$PS))} to \Sexpr{sprintf("%.4f", max(loo.dt$PS))} (all countries: \Sexpr{sprintf("%.4f", loo.full$PS)}), and its p-value is below 0.05 in \Sexpr{sum(loo.dt$PS.p < 0.05)} of the \Sexpr{nrow(loo.dt)} runs. Table \ref{tab:loo} shows the countries whose exclusion changes the contrast the most.

The five countries with most of the logging account for \Sexpr{sprintf("%.0f", 100 * sum(head(logging.by.country$share, 5)))}\% of it (\Sexpr{paste(sprintf("%s %.0f\\%%", latex.escape(head(logging.by.country$country, 5)), 100 * head(logging.by.country$share, 5)), collapse = ", ")}). Leaving out one country at a time, the estimate for L ranges from \Sexpr{sprintf("%.3f", min(loo.L.dt$L))} to \Sexpr{sprintf("%.3f", max(loo.L.dt$L))} IWI units per year per standard deviation (all countries: \Sexpr{sprintf("%.3f", loo.L.full$L)}, p = \Sexpr{sprintf("%.3f", loo.L.full$L.p)}); it is negative in \Sexpr{sum(loo.L.dt$L < 0)} and significant at the 5\% level in \Sexpr{sum(loo.L.dt$L.p < 0.05)} of the \Sexpr{nrow(loo.L.dt)} runs. Table \ref{tab:loo} shows the countries whose exclusion changes the contrast P $-$ S or the estimate for L the most.

<<app.loo.table, results = "asis", cache = FALSE>>=
## leave one country out, model (4): oil palm - shifting cultivation and logging in one table
app.loo <- merge(rbind(loo.full, loo.dt, fill = TRUE)[, list(excluded, areas, PS.ci, PS.p, PS, PS.change = change)],
                 rbind(loo.L.full, loo.L.dt, fill = TRUE)[, list(excluded, L, L.lo, L.hi, L.p, L.change = change)],
                 by = "excluded")
app.loo.show <- unique(c(head(app.loo[order(-PS.change)]$excluded, 5), head(app.loo[order(-L.change)]$excluded, 5)))
app.loo.show <- app.loo.show[!is.na(app.loo.show)]
app.loo.tab <- rbind(app.loo[excluded == "none (all countries)"],
                     app.loo[excluded %in% app.loo.show][order(excluded)])
app.share <- function(x, by) ifelse(x %in% by$country, sprintf("%.0f\\%%", 100 * by$share[match(x, by$country)]), "")
## round the contrast (printed with four decimals by contrast.q2) to three decimals
app.round3 <- function(x) vapply(regmatches(x, gregexpr("-?[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+", x)), function(v)
    sprintf("%.3f [%.3f, %.3f]", as.numeric(v[1]), as.numeric(v[2]), as.numeric(v[3])), "")
app.loo.print <- app.loo.tab[, list(
    `left out` = latex.escape(sub("none (all countries)", "none", excluded, fixed = TRUE)),
    areas = format(areas, big.mark = ","),
    `P share` = app.share(excluded, palm.by.country), `L share` = app.share(excluded, logging.by.country),
    `P $-$ S [95\\% CI]` = app.round3(PS.ci), p = sprintf("%.3f", PS.p),
    `L [95\\% CI]` = sprintf("%.3f [%.3f, %.3f]", L, L.lo, L.hi), ` p` = sprintf("%.3f", L.p))]
xtable::print.xtable(xtable::xtable(app.loo.print,
             caption = "Model (4) of Table \\ref{tab:two-step-country} re-estimated leaving out one country at a time: the full sample (none left out) and the countries whose exclusion changes the contrast P $-$ S or the estimate for L the most. P $-$ S: oil palm $-$ shifting cultivation, IWI units per year per percentage point of the land area. L: logging, IWI units per year per standard deviation. P share and L share: the country's share of all forest loss to oil palm and to logging in the areas of the analysis.",
             label = "tab:loo", align = c("l", ">{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}p{2.3cm}", "r", "r", "r", "r", "r", "r", "r")),
      include.rownames = FALSE, booktabs = TRUE, size = "scriptsize",
      sanitize.text.function = identity, sanitize.colnames.function = identity,
      hline.after = c(-1, 0, 1, nrow(app.loo.print)))
@

Splitting category R into its components (mining and energy, wildfire, other natural disturbances, loss without a dominant driver, the settlements driver where the land is not built-up, and permanent agriculture to other classes) leaves the estimates for the other categories practically unchanged (estimate for S \Sexpr{est.ci.q2(fit.split, "cat_S", sd(q2.dt$cat_S))} per standard deviation, compared with \Sexpr{est.ci.q2(fit.q2.c1, "cat_S", cat.sd[["cat_S"]])} in model 4).

\section{Oil palm by plantation type and planting period}
\label{sec:palm-split}

Of the oil palm planted on former forest land in the areas of the analysis, \Sexpr{sprintf("%.0f", 100 * sum(q2p$p_ind * q2p$valid_2000) / sum(q2p$cat_P * q2p$valid_2000))}\% is industrial and \Sexpr{sprintf("%.0f", 100 * sum(q2p$p_sm * q2p$valid_2000) / sum(q2p$cat_P * q2p$valid_2000))}\% smallholder; \Sexpr{palm.n[["p_ind"]]} areas have more than 1\% of their land in industrial oil palm and \Sexpr{palm.n[["p_sm"]]} in smallholder oil palm.

Oil palm with a planting year but not in the 2021 extent map (``untyped'', \Sexpr{sprintf("%.0f", 100 * sum(q2p$p_un * q2p$valid_2000) / sum(q2p$cat_P * q2p$valid_2000))}\% of P) occurs almost only together with smallholder oil palm (correlation \Sexpr{sprintf("%.2f", cor(q2p$p_un, q2p$p_sm.only))} across areas), most likely small smallholder fields that the 10 m map did not detect, so it is counted as smallholder oil palm. To separate the maturity of the plantations from long-established oil palm economies, the models also control for oil palm planted before 2001 (on any land).

<<app.palm.split.table, results = "asis", cache = FALSE>>=
## as chunk palm.split.table, without model (9)
app.palm.specs <- palm.specs[c("type", "period")]
app.palm.tab <- rbindlist(lapply(names(app.palm.specs), function(m) {
    vars <- c(app.palm.specs[[m]], "cat_S")
    data.table(model = c(type = "(7) by type", period = "(8) by period")[[m]],
               variable = palm.labels[vars], areas = palm.n[vars],
               est = sapply(vars, function(v) est.ci.pp(palm.fits[[m]]$fit, v)))
}))
app.palm.tab[duplicated(model), model := ""]
setnames(app.palm.tab, c("model", "", "areas $>$ 1\\%", "per pp [95\\% CI]"))
xtable::print.xtable(xtable::xtable(app.palm.tab,
             caption = paste0("Oil palm on former forest land by plantation type and planting period: difference in the observed IWI trend (IWI units per year) per percentage point of the land area, comparing areas within the same country, with 95\\% confidence intervals clustered by country. ",
                              format(nobs(palm.fits$type$fit), big.mark = ","), " areas. Same controls and the other categories of forest loss as in Table \\ref{tab:two-step-country}, and oil palm planted before 2001; $\\hat\\tau_{\\mathrm{res}}$ = ",
                              paste(sprintf("%.3f", sqrt(sapply(palm.fits[c("type", "period")], `[[`, "tau2"))), collapse = " and "),
                              " for models (7) and (8). Untyped oil palm is counted as smallholder. Areas $>$ 1\\%: areas with more than 1\\% of the land area in the category."),
             label = "tab:palm-split", align = c("l", "l", "l", "r", "r")),
      include.rownames = FALSE, booktabs = TRUE, size = "footnotesize",
      sanitize.text.function = identity, sanitize.colnames.function = identity,
      hline.after = c(-1, 0, cumsum(lengths(app.palm.specs) + 1)))
@

\section{Effect of forest loss per country}
\label{app:rq1-countries}

<<rq1b.country.table, results = "asis">>=
library(data.table); library(xtable)
rq1c.n <- rq1.long[, list(areas = uniqueN(area.name), regions = uniqueN(top.level.region)), by = country]
rq1c.tab <- merge(rq1b.country, rq1c.n, by = "country")[order(-est)]
rq1c.print <- rq1c.tab[, list(Country = country, Regions = regions, Areas = areas,
                              `Effect [95\\% CI]` = sprintf("%.3f [%.3f, %.3f]", est,
                                                            est - qnorm(0.975) * se, est + qnorm(0.975) * se),
                              ` ` = ifelse(sig == "positive", "+", ifelse(sig == "negative", "$-$", "")))]
print(xtable(rq1c.print,
             caption = "Effect of forest loss on the average IWI of an area per country, IWI units per percentage point of forest cover lost since 2000: the average effect plus the deviation of the country, with approximate 95\\% intervals, from the model in Figure \\ref{fig:rq1b.map.region}. $+$ and $-$: the interval lies above or below zero. Regions and areas: number of top level regions and areas with interviews 2000--2020.",
             label = "tab:rq1-countries", align = c("l", "l", "r", "r", "r", "c")),
      include.rownames = FALSE, booktabs = TRUE, size = "footnotesize", tabular.environment = "longtable",
      floating = FALSE, sanitize.text.function = identity, sanitize.colnames.function = identity)
@

